  </section>

  <section class="band band--surface" id="verify" aria-labelledby="verify-title">
    <div class="container downloads__install">
      <SectionHead
        id="verify-title"
        label="Verify a Core-Geth download"
        title="Verify what you downloaded"
        em="Verify"
        size="medium"
      >
        <p class="lede">
          Two checks, and they answer different questions. The checksum says the file arrived
          intact. The attestation says which workflow built it, from which commit, which is the
          question a checksum cannot answer.
        </p>
      </SectionHead>
      <PlatformTabs
        legend="Choose your platform for checking a download"
        panels={VERIFY_PANELS}
        prefix="verify-"
      />
      <div class="downloads__group">
        <p>
          The attestation check also prints the policy it enforced, which the blocks leave out: the
          repository, the workflow identity and the OIDC issuer it requires before it will succeed.
        </p>
        <h3>What every release carries</h3>
        <ul class="plain" role="list">
          <li>
            <strong>A checksum beside every file.</strong> A <code>.sha256</code> is published with
            each archive and each image tarball.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A build attestation on every archive and image.</strong> It is minted against a
            short-lived certificate issued to the workflow run itself, and records which workflow,
            at which commit, produced the file, so each one traces to the run that built it rather
            than resting on anyone's word.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Provenance for the container images.</strong> Recorded for the published image
            index and for each image tarball.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          On a first run Windows names the publisher as unknown, and macOS blocks a downloaded
          binary until it is allowed. The <a href={DOCS.installation.href}>installation guide</a>{" "}
          says how to check an archive and proceed.
        </p>
        <a class="more" href={`${DOCS_URL}audits/2026-09-release-pipeline/`}>
          <ArrowText text="How the release artifacts are built and checked" />
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<script is:inline>
  // The copy button on each code block, and the only script this site ships beyond the
  // JSON-LD in the head. Maintainer decision, 2026-10-07.
  //
  // It is `is:inline` so Astro leaves it in the page rather than bundling it to a file:
  // one small script, no extra request, and `dist` stays free of JavaScript files. The
  // button is created here rather than rendered in the markup, so a reader with no
  // JavaScript, or a browser without the clipboard API, sees no button that cannot work.
  (function () {
    if (!navigator.clipboard) return;
    for (const wrap of document.querySelectorAll(".shell-wrap")) {
      const code = wrap.querySelector("pre.shell > code");
      if (!code) continue;
      const button = document.createElement("button");
      button.type = "button";
      button.className = "copy";
      button.textContent = "Copy";
      // The label is the button's text, so a change of state is announced rather than
      // hidden behind a fixed aria-label.
      button.setAttribute("aria-live", "polite");
      let reset;
      button.addEventListener("click", function () {
        navigator.clipboard.writeText(code.textContent).then(
          function () {
            button.textContent = "Copied";
            button.classList.add("copy--done");
          },
          function () {
            button.textContent = "Copy failed";
          },
        );
        clearTimeout(reset);
        reset = setTimeout(function () {
          button.textContent = "Copy";
          button.classList.remove("copy--done");
        }, 2000);
      });
      wrap.appendChild(button);
    }
  })();
</script>

